{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Why\nWritable container layers are each container's overlay2 `upperdir`. They grow independently of\nimage layers and build cache, no prune reaches them, and nothing measured them on 2026-09-02 —\nthe only signal was \"root disk high\". The last cap under ADR 0112.\n\n## Mechanism decision, with evidence\nThe ticket offers a runtime storage quota \"when compatible\", else a reaper. **The quota is NOT\ncompatible here.** overlay2's `--storage-opt size=` (and the `overlay2.size` daemon default) is\naccepted only when the filesystem backing `/var/lib/docker` is XFS mounted with `pquota`, else\nthe daemon refuses: `--storage-opt is supported only for overlay over xfs with 'pquota' mount\noption`. That filesystem is ROOT here, and XFS reads quota options at MOUNT time and refuses\nthem on a remount — so it needs `rootflags=pquota` and a REBOOT: the constraint S4 (`2ba3`) hit\nfor `/var/tmp`. Even then it is PER-CONTAINER, not an aggregate ceiling, and applies at\ncreation, so every live service must be recreated. So this ships the mitigation, publishes which\nregime the box is in, and documents how to enable it.\n\n## What the reaper does NOT guarantee\n1. **It cannot reclaim a RUNNING container's writable layer at all.** Only exited/dead ones are\n   removable, so for the live compose set this is MEASUREMENT AND AN ALARM, not a bound; only\n   the reboot-gated quota would bound those. Sharper than S4's fill rate, so it is said first.\n2. For the debris it does bound, the bound is `cap + fill_rate x interval`: at 2 GiB and 300 s a\n   sustained net fill above ~7.2 MB/s outruns it, and this gp3 volume does 125 MB/s (~17x).\n3. Nothing that exited inside the 900 s grace window is removed; when it cannot get under the\n   share it SAYS SO.\n\n## Safety — proved against the live set\n`docker container prune --filter until=…` cannot express what this host needs: its filters are\n`until` and `label` only, with NO name filter. `autodeploy.sh` starts the mcp blue-green backends\nwith a bare `docker run` compose never sees and reaps them in `mcp_retire_sweep`, under a guard\nthis reaper cannot replicate: it reads the nginx `/mcp/` upstream include and refuses to reap an\nexited container still named as the live backend, since `--restart always` would have restored\nit. Reaping one anyway is bug `9ea3`: a transient exit became a permanent 502 on 2026-09-02. So\ncandidates are removed one by one, sparing:\n\n* `com.docker.compose.project`-labelled (gerrit, review-bot, opcert, compose-mcp-1): an exited\n  compose service is a crashed one whose logs are the evidence;\n* `rebar.service`-labelled (`mcp_run_new`'s stable identity);\n* matching `^(rebar-mcp|compose-mcp-1)` — `mcp_managed`'s regex, so the two agree.\n\nTwo guarantees the DAEMON enforces, not shell logic: candidates come from `docker ps -a --filter\nstatus=exited --filter status=dead`, which never lists a running container, and removal is\n`docker rm` WITHOUT `-f`, which the daemon refuses for a running one. No `-v`/`--volumes`, and\n`docker system prune` is never used.\n\n**Sizes and termination.** ONE `docker inspect --size` gives the total and each candidate's\n`SizeRw` — the same field `docker system df` sums into its Containers row, so census and ledger\ncannot disagree. The loop sorts by `State.FinishedAt` ASCENDING (oldest exit first) and\nSUBTRACTS each removed size from its running total, stopping at 80% of the share; it never\nre-reads the ledger per removal, which could not converge under concurrent writes.\n\n## What invokes the reaper, and what publishes the heartbeat\nTwo DIFFERENT callers; naming one is how a heartbeat ends up with no producer. The REAPER runs\nfrom its own `rebar-container-reaper.timer` (5 min, `TimeoutStartSec` nested below the period\nper bug `1205`), rendered by `--print-units` and installed by `compose-up.sh` beside the three\ncaps it already installs — a timer rather than an autodeploy hook, since `prune_docker_caches`\nruns only on deploy/backoff/pressure paths. The HEARTBEAT comes from\n`observability.sh`'s existing 5-minute timer, whose §2i calls the side-effect-free\n`--check-active` — so \"every tick\" is the OBSERVABILITY tick, and the heartbeat still has a\nproducer when the reaper timer is the dead thing.\n\n## Observability (S2's publish-or-stay-silent rule)\n§2i, from the SAME single `docker system df` call §2f already makes — its format string gains\n`{{.Reclaimable}}`, not a second daemon walk. Three DF-DERIVED readings:\n`container_writable_bytes` (Containers Size), `container_exited_bytes` (Containers Reclaimable)\nand `container_writable_used_percent` (`pct_of_cap`, S3's helper, reused). Plus two 1/0\nALWAYS-PUBLISH STATE METRICS: `container_reaper_active` (is the timer in force — the heartbeat)\nand `container_quota_enforceable` (is the kernel enforcing XFS project quota on the Docker root\nfs). The state metrics publish on EVERY tick including their 0 path; the df-derived three only\non a parseable Containers row.\n\n**Field surface verified — MATCH** against the CLI reference: `{{.Reclaimable}}` exists, and on\nthe Containers row `Size` sums writable layers (`SizeRw`) while `Reclaimable` is the non-running\nsubset. A missing/unparseable field publishes NOTHING — silence, never a wrong number.\n\n## Alarms (`monitoring_autodeploy.tf`)\n`rebar-container-writable-usage-high` (>85%) and `rebar-container-reaper-not-active` (<1).\n**The second pre-empts the S4 finding**: without it a silently-dead timer leaves usage nominal\nwhile nothing enforces anything. Both `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, both SNS-wired.\n`container_quota_enforceable` gets NO alarm: it reads 0 until the reboot, and a permanently\nfiring alarm is muted within a day.\n\n## Scope\n`docker-storage-cap.sh` owns the BUDGET, `container-cap.sh` only the reaper/checks/rendering.\nADR 0112 puts this share INSIDE the Docker budget, so the former gains\n`DOCKER_CONTAINER_WRITABLE_BYTES` (2 GiB, a decision-6 starting point) carved out of the\nexisting 20 GiB — BuildKit 5 + this 2 + images 13 = 20, images having been 15 — and\n`container-cap.sh` READS it, so no second literal drifts.\n\nIn: NEW `infra/scripts/container-cap.sh`; `docker-storage-cap.sh` (third share);\n`observability.sh` (§2i + extended parse); `compose-up.sh` (installs the timer);\n`monitoring_autodeploy.tf`; `infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md`;\n`tests/scripts/test_docker_storage_cap.py` (its `DOCKER_IMAGE_SHARE_BYTES` assertions move to\nthe three-way split); `tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py`; two NEW test files.\nOut: overlay2 deletion, the S2/S3/S4 caps, gate scratch.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `--reap` removes an exited unowned container over the share, nothing under it (tested at\n      90% of the share, i.e. between the low-water mark and the ceiling), reaps oldest-exit-first\n      to the 80% mark, and WARNs naming the running/protected bytes when it cannot get under.\n- [ ] `--reap` removes nothing for each of: a `running` row; a `com.docker.compose.project` or\n      `rebar.service` label; a `rebar-mcp-*` / `compose-mcp-1` name; an exit inside the grace\n      window; an unreadable share. Its argv never contains `-f`, `-v`, `--volumes`,\n      `system prune` or `container prune`; a test pins the name pattern to `mcp_managed`'s.\n- [ ] `observability.sh` publishes the three df-derived readings from a parseable Containers row\n      and NONE when absent or unparseable; percent clamped to 100; the state metrics publish\n      every tick including their 0 path; all five dimensionless.\n- [ ] `docker-storage-cap.sh --print-env` states the third share, the three sum to the budget\n      (5+2+13=20 GiB), and an over-subscribed split is refused.\n- [ ] Both alarms exist by name, `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` and SNS-wired, nothing\n      alarms on `container_quota_enforceable`, and `terraform fmt -check`/`validate` pass.\n- [ ] The runbook documents diagnosis, remediation, the `rootflags=pquota` + reboot steps, and\n      forbids manual overlay2 deletion.\n- [ ] Portability: Docker APIs, systemd, local pytest; no CI-provider trigger."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788595851376128001,"uuid":"28599d77-07a2-4c86-8ac1-146dc9776378"}